  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nSprawling surface high pressure anchored just north of the Great\nLakes has resulted in another morning of clear skies amid a \nvery dry lower to middle atmosphere. In fact, 00Z regional \nRAOBs resemble something more typical of that seen across the \nhigh plains than the central Appalachians. This has allowed \ntemperatures once again to quickly fall into the 30s and low \n40s across much of the area, amid some developing river valley \nsteam fog along the Ohio River and points south. Expect a slow \nincrease in fog coverage through dawn, quite similar to that of\nyesterday, perhaps slightly more widespread given current \ntrends, with locally dense fog expected in the 'typical' areas. \nLows this morning will range from the mid 20s across the \nmountain valley cold spots, with 30s expected most elsewhere. A \nFrost Advisory remains in effect until 9 AM this morning across \nmuch of southeast Ohio and the central/northern lowlands of WV. \nA SPS is in effect for our remaining zones where the growing \nseason is still active, but overall, coverage is expected to be \nmuch more patchy here given more river valley fog and dew points\nthat didn't mix down as low Monday afternoon compared to \nlocations further north. \n\nA persistence pattern remains in place over the next few days \nas high pressure prevails across the region. Soundings continue \nto depict a very dry layer near H850, which will likely mix \ndown to an extent during the afternoons, with dew point drops \nMonday afternoon nearing a very impressive 15 degrees is some \nlocations. Given such, have lowered afternoon dew points over \nthe next few days, with minimum afternoon RH values generally in\nthe 20s/30s, but amid very light surface flow. Near seasonable \ndaytime temperatures are expected, with mid 60s to 70 or so \nacross the lowlands amid mostly sunny skies. A weak and dry backdoor\ncold front will attempt to slide through the region sometime on\nThursday, potentially resulting in temperatures a few degrees \ncooler through Saturday. Return downsloping flow will result in\na gradual warming trend on Sunday/Monday.\n\nThe very dry low/mid levels will continue to facilitate \nefficient radiational cooling throughout the work week. Given \nsuch, have continued the trend of lowering overnight temperatures\nfrom central guidance a fair amount towards COOP MOS, with \nfairly widespread low temperatures in the 30s expected through \nThursday morning. Patchy frost will once again be possible\ntonight, and perhaps more so Wednesday night. Given anticipated\nlower coverage tonight, will not issue any headlines at this \npoint and will allow day/evening shift to re-evaluate given \nafternoon and evening T/Td trends. \n\nOvernight and early morning river valley steam fog is expected\nto persist throughout the work week and weekend given a \ncontinued large air / water temperature difference, with fog \nlocally dense in spots.",
